#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# legendre_transform

#% text
# `legendre_transform(L, q, p, t)` gives the Hamiltonian `sum p q' - L` of the Lagrangian `L` in the coordinates `q` and the momenta `p`, with the velocities solved from `p == dL/dq'`. `legendre_transform(f, x, p)` gives the Legendre transform `p x - f(x)` of a function, with `x` solved from `p == f'(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The momenta `p` are names (a list of names, one per coordinate); the coordinates keep the form they have in `L`.

#% item
# A singular Lagrangian, whose velocities cannot be solved for uniquely (one linear in the velocities, for example), has no Legendre transform: the call stays unevaluated.

#% item
# For `legendre_transform(f, x, p)` the first solution of `p == f'(x)` is used, with an integer constant of the solution set to 0.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
legendre_transform(m*x'(t)^2/2 - k*x(t)^2/2, x, p, t)
#% output
#= (1/2)*k*x(t)^2 + p^2/(2*m)

#% cell
legendre_transform(m*(r'(t)^2 + r(t)^2*th'(t)^2)/2 - V(r(t)), [r, th], [pr, pth], t)
#% output
#= V(r(t)) + pr^2/(2*m) + pth^2/(2*m*r(t)^2)

#% text
# Minimal coupling to a vector potential gives `(p - e A)^2/(2 m)`:

#% cell
legendre_transform(m*x'(t)^2/2 + e0*x'(t)*A(x(t)), x, p, t)
#% output
#= p^2/(2*m) + e0^2*A(x(t))^2/(2*m) - e0*p*A(x(t))/m

#% text
# A singular Lagrangian:

#% cell
legendre_transform(x'(t)*y(t), [x, y], [p1, p2], t)
#% output
#= legendre_transform(y(t)*x'(t), [x, y], [p1, p2], t)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
legendre_transform(x^2/2, x, p)
#% output
#= (1/2)*p^2

#% cell
legendre_transform(exp(x), x, p)
#% output
#= -p + p*log(p)

